[{"result":{"data":{"json":{"id":"1265","stub":{"id":"1265","section":"2. Route 28N to Hudson River","altname":"","river":"Boreas River","difficulty":"IIItoV","states":["USA-NYO"]},"primaryGaugeStatus":{"status":"below-recommended","latestReading":{"value":"1640","dateTime":"2026-05-12T02:15:00.000Z","qualifier":"NORMAL"},"metric":"cfs","adjustedDifficulty":null},"pointOfInterests":[{"type":"put-in","description":null,"id":"110135","location":{"latitude":"43.8918113708496","longitude":"-74.0151596069336"},"name":"Put In","distance":"0","photoID":null,"difficulty":"N/A","approximate":false},{"type":"rapid","description":"After an initial warm-up of gently moving water, the river turns sharply left and tilts downhill. Class III/IV whitewater has an eddy on the lower left; you may want to catch this eddy and scout around the next bend. The river turns sharply to the right, narrows, and ends in a hole which can usually be punched through. The waterfall is several hundred yards downstream.","id":"6031","location":{"latitude":"0","longitude":"0"},"name":"Entrance Exam","distance":"0.5","difficulty":"IV","approximate":false,"photoID":"0"},{"type":"hazard","description":"This is about a 10 foot waterfall. It is vertical on the right and sloping in the center. It is a double drop on the extreme left, next to the undercut wall.","id":"6032","location":{"latitude":"0","longitude":"0"},"name":"Waterfall","distance":"1","photoID":"0","difficulty":"V","approximate":true},{"type":"rapid","description":"After several miles of flatwater, the river tilts noticably downward. After about a mile of class III fun, there is a large eddy on river left. You may want to catch the eddy and catch your breath; this is where the nonstop class IV action begins. The river is steeper and twisting from here to the Northwoods Club bridge. Swimming is to be avoided.\n\nThe name was originally coined by Dennis Squires, who wrote a whitewater guidebook and perished on a river in New Zealand.","id":"6034","location":{"latitude":"0","longitude":"0"},"name":"Guts & Glory","distance":"4","difficulty":"N/A","approximate":true,"photoID":"0"},{"type":"takeout","description":null,"id":"115395","location":{"latitude":"43.7440605163574","longitude":"-74.057975769043"},"name":"Take Out","distance":"8","photoID":null,"difficulty":"N/A","approximate":false}],"alerts":[{"id":"101252653","title":null,"post_date":"2019-05-01 04:00:00+00","detail":"Massive strainer on/in the river left route around the island just above the Northwoods Club Road take-out. It is believed by this author that no whitewater vessel can pass through and/or under this strainer, all whitewater enthusiasts should stay right at the large island at the end of the guts and glory rapid. Strainer was seen mid to late August, 2016","user":{"name":"TJ King"}},{"id":"101246657","title":null,"post_date":"2010-03-28 04:00:00+00","detail":"Ran from Route 28N to North River on the Hudson.\nThe wire is indeed gone.","user":{"name":"North Creek Rafting Company"}},{"id":"101142415","title":null,"post_date":"2009-10-24 04:00:00+00","detail":"In 2008 there was a wire across the river about a mile below the Northwoods Club Rd. bridge. It was skipping right on the surface of the water and almost cleared us out of our raft, luckily we were left of center so we had a little clearance. We were looking for it, but thought it was removed because we remembered it to be closer to the bridge, and then BAM!! there it was.\n\n10-26-09 - A group that ran the Boreas to the Hudson reported the wire was gone. (A group of Guides took care of that)","user":null}],"photos":[{"id":"873227","ext":"jpeg","post_id":"873228","description":"Nate Pelton running a 14' cataraft in Guts & Glory. The Northwoods Club Road bridge gauge was at 0.","caption":"Cataraft in Guts & Glory","poi_name":null,"subject":null,"author":null,"poi_id":null,"photo_date":null,"updated_at":null,"url":{"medium":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/medium/873227","large":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/big/873227"}},{"id":"nbT6l93NwjiQuXpzJZ1Rx","ext":"jpg","post_id":"f2AzQzoJIscBMDg4ImuCj","description":"","caption":"Boreas","poi_name":null,"subject":"","author":"Noah Pollock","poi_id":null,"photo_date":"2025-05-06T00:45:31.000Z","updated_at":"2025-05-06T00:45:31.000Z","url":{"medium":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/medium/nbT6l93NwjiQuXpzJZ1Rx","large":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/big/nbT6l93NwjiQuXpzJZ1Rx"}},{"id":"40736","ext":"jpeg","post_id":"9928","description":"","caption":"40736.jpeg","poi_name":"","subject":"","author":"","poi_id":null,"photo_date":null,"updated_at":null,"url":{"medium":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/medium/40736","large":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/big/40736"}},{"id":"40737","ext":"jpeg","post_id":"9928","description":"","caption":"40737.jpeg","poi_name":"","subject":"","author":"","poi_id":null,"photo_date":null,"updated_at":null,"url":{"medium":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/medium/40737","large":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/big/40737"}},{"id":"40808","ext":"jpeg","post_id":"9929","description":"","caption":"Boreas Waterfall Scout","poi_name":"Waterfall","subject":"Nelson Miller","author":"Rick Morse","poi_id":"6032","photo_date":null,"updated_at":null,"url":{"medium":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/medium/40808","large":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/big/40808"}},{"id":"40803","ext":"jpeg","post_id":"9929","description":"","caption":"Boreas Waterfall","poi_name":"Waterfall","subject":"","author":"Rick Morse","poi_id":"6032","photo_date":null,"updated_at":null,"url":{"medium":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/medium/40803","large":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/big/40803"}}],"detail":{"id":"1265","permitUrl":null,"permitInfo":null,"description":"The Boreas River is a classic Northcountry river.\n\nJust below the route 28N bridge put-in, the water flows peacefully though a boreal forest. The pace picks up, and suddenly you find yourself in a challenging zig-zag rapid (IV) which constricts into an exit hydrologic. Immediately downstream is a waterfall (V), which can be scouted (and/or carried) on the right.\n\nMinor rapids lead into a substantial section of flatwater. Just when you're beginning to wonder when it will end, the riverbed tilts downward. Get ready, because the next several miles has some of the most continuous creeking rapids (IV) in the Adirondacks. When you reach the North Woods Club Road bridge, you can take-out here, or continue another mile of rapids (II) to the Hudson River.\n\nIf you're not into the continuous set of Class IV rapids, you might be interested in the [Upper Boreas](http://www.americanwhitewater.org/content/River_detail_id_1266_).","averageGradient":"0","maxGradient":"0","length":"13","correlations":[{"reachID":"1265","isPrimary":true,"correlationDetails":{"metric":"cfs","belowRecommendedRangeComment":null,"lowRunnableRangeComment":null,"mediumRunnableRangeComment":null,"highRunnableRangeComment":null,"aboveRecommendedRangeComment":null,"lowRunnableAdjustedDifficulty":null,"highRunnableAdjustedDifficulty":null,"beginLowRunnable":"5000","beginMediumRunnable":null,"beginHighRunnable":null,"endHighRunnable":"20000"},"gaugeInfo":{"name":"HUDSON RIVER AT NORTH CREEK NY","gaugeSource":"USGS","gaugeSourceIdentifier":"01315500","gaugeFetchStatus":"ACTIVE","latestFlowReading":{"value":"1640","dateTime":"2026-05-12T02:15:00.000Z","qualifier":"NORMAL"},"latestStageReading":{"value":"4.17","dateTime":"2026-05-12T02:15:00.000Z","qualifier":"NORMAL"},"location":{"latitude":"43.7008611","longitude":"-73.9831944"},"externalSourceLinks":{"sourceLink":{"url":"https://water.noaa.gov/gauges/01315500","text":"NOAA"},"secondaryLink":{"url":"https://waterdata.usgs.gov/monitoring-location/01315500/#parameterCode=00060&period=P7D","text":"US Geological Survey"}}},"migrationErrorExplanation":null,"comment":"This gauge is on a nearby stream, so it should be used as an indicator only.","status":{"status":"below-recommended","latestReading":{"value":"1640","dateTime":"2026-05-12T02:15:00.000Z","qualifier":"NORMAL"},"metric":"cfs","adjustedDifficulty":null}}],"pointOfInterestIDs":["110135","6031","6032","6034","115395"],"geometry":{"type":"LineString","coordinates":[[-74.01514903960815,43.89179474350749],[-74.01518912888724,43.89176926520457],[-74.01496359555426,43.890844998539315],[-74.01498679555425,43.89060033187303],[-74.01511559555405,43.88998459854065],[-74.01544032888688,43.88955399854132],[-74.0156011955533,43.88931219854169],[-74.0158463955529,43.88885059854243],[-74.01606419555259,43.888725065209314],[-74.01612152888583,43.88864559854272],[-74.01620012888571,43.88853026520957],[-74.01636392888543,43.88839119854316],[-74.01643352888533,43.88800526521039],[-74.0163167955522,43.886622798545886],[-74.01632579555218,43.88618566521325],[-74.01645752888527,43.88562299854743],[-74.01660932888507,43.88525973188133],[-74.01672912888489,43.88498166521509],[-74.01696012888453,43.88473713188216],[-74.01721299555078,43.88452539854916],[-74.01758712888352,43.884208598549606],[-74.01841219554893,43.88324139855115],[-74.01846272888218,43.88303999855145],[-74.0184947955488,43.88291219855165],[-74.01850506221547,43.882456931885656],[-74.01842152888224,43.88226446521929],[-74.01800699554957,43.88196319855314],[-74.01777899554992,43.88151339855381],[-74.01772532888333,43.88131919855414],[-74.01791632888302,43.881146731887725],[-74.01846692888216,43.88080993188822],[-74.01884712888159,43.88052039855535],[-74.01909292888121,43.88039233188891],[-74.01932839554752,43.88033266522234],[-74.01983712888007,43.88027246522239],[-74.0204182622125,43.8802273985558],[-74.02101706221157,43.880252598555785],[-74.02139919554429,43.880340731888964],[-74.02170266221049,43.8803362652223],[-74.02186099554359,43.88026359855576],[-74.0219504622101,43.88